WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.820
The booster 23's rollout to massi for cryo testing keeps the next starship hardware cycle on schedule. This is SpaceX Daily Episode 129. The super heavy booster arrived at the starbase Texas test site on October 13th, according to observers posting on X, acclaimed the company has not itself confirmed. The proof fills the tanks with liquid oxygen and liquid methane to confirm they hold together before any engine firing. Stacking and flight prep wait on that result, and those observers already have the

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.940
vehicle set for tanking. Elon Musk told roughly 2,000 people at Giga Texas on Saturday that

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:41.520
Flight 15 remains four to six weeks away and will likely fly just before Thanksgiving, keep track

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.920
reports. He was at the X takeover event and the change list is shorter than on recent flights,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.920
though some modifications still mean going inside the tanks. Flight 16 is planned to fly both the

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:58.560
super heavy booster and the ship upper stage again. Starship version 3 is aiming at 100 metric

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.600
tons to orbit fully reusable, and that mass only works if the ship comes home intact.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.600
Flight 15 will try to catch the ship upper stage with the tower arms.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:10.600
Super heavy boosters have been caught several times.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.900
A starship upper stage has never been caught, and every earlier ship has ended in the ocean.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:23.120
Falcon 9 booster B10888 left Space Launch Complex 4 East at Vandenberg on Flight 20 with the

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.020
Starlink 15-25 mission.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:31.780
It lofted 27 V2 mini-optimized satellites toward a 70-degree orbit, Space.com reports.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.680
That was the first Starlink launch since mid-September, a gap of more than three weeks.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:45.160
SpaceX has now launched 12,988 Starlink satellites in total, with 11,131 still in orbit

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.920
and 11,117 operational.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.840
Bandenberg Space Force Base logged at 69th launch of the year on this flight.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:02:06.060
Cargo Dragon C-211 then rose on Falcon 9 booster B-1096, the booster's eighth flight and the capsule's fourth, after CRS-26, CRS-29 and CRS-33.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.680
Lift off from Space Launch Complex 40 was targeted for 6.33 a.m. Eastern. Keep track reports.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.300
Docking is planned at Harmony Zenith port, the top port, around 1500 UTC on October 15.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:24.900
The spacecraft will stay docked for about a month, completing SpaceX's 35th commercial

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.400
resupply services mission.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.660
That port opened up after Crew Dragon Freedom brought the 4 Crew 12 astronauts home on

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.140
October 8th.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:38.360
EON Space Labs, a Hyderabad startup, reached orbit with its Mira Earth observation payload

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.600
on Falcon 9 Transporter 18 on October 2nd.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:42.840
Your story reports.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.600
First images are expected within 10 days, and the company holds an Indian patent for Mira.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:55.480
It built germanium-free long-wave infrared lenses, LWIR, the thermal band, from synthetically

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.760
grown domestic materials, cutting lead times from six months to six weeks.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:04.340
The firm is working with more than seven space customers and has interest from roughly

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.720
120 companies for its aerial EON IR systems.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:13.820
A handheld imager combining short-wave infrared, long-wave infrared, and day cameras has already

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:19.240
been trialled on Indian Navy ships, spotting vessels at around 30 km in low visibility.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:24.640
Eight upcoming Starlink conjunctions, close approaches with other objects, sit on the

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:30.280
Socrates High Probability List, Keep Track Reports, none of those events is rated high-risk.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.120
One report is flagged as suspect because the upstream numbers are physically implausible,

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.600
and it is treated as stale or erroneous.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.560
Separately, one Starlink satellite carries an active reentry prediction and is expected

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.920
to come down in the coming days as normal end-of-life disposal.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.960
Controlled re-entries limit debris and keep the constellation inside licensed orbital

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:51.360
shells.

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:57.200
Reddit users in the SpaceX lounge forum discussed a recurring United Launch Alliance advertisement

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:02.300
on the NASA Space Flight Starbase live feed, and whether that placement spoke to competitive

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:02.920
positioning.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.700
The thread turned on how live stream sponsorships sit next to industry rivalries.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.300
In Mount Pleasant, local coverage noted that residents can watch the upcoming Florida

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.400
a dragon launch, while a new conveyor belt sushi restaurant opened nearby, the Post and

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:22.260
Courier reports. The piece put the launch beside an everyday commercial opening as a visible

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:28.000
community event. Worth keeping an eye on is a reported $650 million insurance gap for

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.600
early starship cargo flights, because current policies cannot cover the full value of those payloads,

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:39.740
Benzinger reports. Insurers cite limited flight heritage and high replacement costs for large orbital

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:45.340
assets. Closing that gap would take demonstrated reliability through repeated successful recoveries,

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:50.360
or new risk-sharing structures with launch customers. For a super-heavy tank set,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.640
the engineering reality of cryogenic proof is a controlled chill toward liquid oxygen temperature,

00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:03.240
while crews watch for leaks and deformation. Stainless 304L shrinks about 0.3% linearly from room

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:10.700
temperature near 293 kelvin down to 90 kelvin, a drop of roughly 200 kelvin, austenitic stainless

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:16.720
moves at about 17 millions of strain per kelvin, and that coefficient times the drop is the contraction.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:23.760
Liquid methane in service sits near 111 kelvin, so the oxygen side chill is the harsher thermal step

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:29.780
on the steel. A cylinder that shortens and pinches inward loads hoop stress, tension around the belt,

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:34.960
belt, pressure times diameter over twice the wall thickness, and longitudinal stress along

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.380
the length, and every weld and flange must stay below yield.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:44.060
The proof pressurizes the tank while it is cold, so a microcrack that only opens at flight

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:45.740
temperature shows up on the stand.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.480
Past that gate and the vehicle can take an ambient proof on the second tank, then a combined

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:56.660
tanking that loads both propellants at once against the common bulkhead, the shared wall between

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:02.460
oxygen and methane, and the feed line interfaces. Each clean cycle lowers the odds of a thermal

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:07.900
leak on the first hot fire, because the friction stir welds, which join metal without melting it,

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.900
and the flange seals have already seen flight light cold.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.740
Strain gauges from the test also retune the finite element models, the computer stress maps,

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.700
that predict fatigue across the targeted 10-plus flights per booster.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:25.540
The magic wand inputs are stainless plate, weld wire, and forming energy.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:31.440
The idiot index is how many times the finished tank costs over those inputs, and a back-of-envelope

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.200
figure for large welded aerospace tanks often lands on the order of 10 to 20 times, mostly

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.960
because a pad leak can take out the vehicle and the stand.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.640
Proof testing collapses that ratio by catching defects before the pad, and because the same

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.640
tanks are meant for 10-plus flights, each extra cycle spreads that inspection cost across

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:51.740
the reuse program.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:58.720
LNG land storage sees the same 304L contraction problem, but those tanks are not asked to fly,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:04.760
land, and chill again. When the booster later returns, the same cold sequence can run at lower pressure

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:11.360
to confirm fatigue has not opened new leak paths. Any change in weld schedule or flange preload has to

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.740
be rechecked at 90 Kelvin, which is how the per flight cost of the booster stays low as the flight

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:23.740
count climbs. On the session, SPC-X finished roughly flat. Before we go, Thanksgiving is the window

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:29.000
now on the first attempt to catch a ship. That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

